\paragraph{Relationship to concurrent credit methods.}
Our dual-mechanism framework provides a lens for analyzing concurrent methods. HCAPO~\cite{hcapo2026} addresses M2 through hindsight credit assignment but retains windowed context ($h{=}2$), leaving M1 unresolved; similarly, GiGPO~\cite{feng2025gigpo} improves step-level advantage via anchor-state grouping without expanding observation scope. Our framework predicts these methods will be effective when M2 dominates but may remain bounded by M1 on tasks requiring long-range context. Episode-level training addresses both mechanisms simultaneously, suggesting complementary strengths depending on task structure.

